<article class="env-definition" id="003B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/003B">Definition <span data-tag="003B">4.13.1</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category and let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of $\mathcal{C}$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say that $f$ is a <em>monomorphism</em> if for every object $W$ and every pair of morphisms $a, b : W \to X$ such that $f \circ a = f \circ b$ we have $a = b$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $f$ is an <em>epimorphism</em> if for every object $W$ and every pair of morphisms $a, b : Y \to W$ such that $a \circ f = b \circ f$ we have $a = b$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>